From: Luke Johnson <jaewun@me.com>
Date: Mon, 6 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +1200
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm: dump the display snapshot to the console

The display snapshot is the driver's own record of the DPU and DSI
hardware state at the moment something went wrong, and it is captured
from two places:

	dpu_encoder.c:  first dpu_encoder_frame_done_timeout() of the boot
	msm_kms.c:      first msm_kms_fault_handler() invocation

But MSM_DISP_SNAPSHOT_DUMP_IN_CONSOLE is 0, so _msm_disp_snapshot_work()
never prints anything.  The capture is handed to dev_coredumpm() and
nothing else, which means on a normal system the snapshot exists for a
few seconds and is then consumed and removed by systemd-coredump.  On
the AYN Thor a devcoredump entry (devcd3, failing_device
ae00000.display-subsystem/ae01000.display-controller) was observed and
was gone before it could be read.

That makes the one diagnostic that fires at the exact instant of a stall
unusable, which in practice has left the display fault undiagnosable:
the only way to get the registers is to read
/sys/kernel/debug/dri/0/kms, which has to race the fault.

Print the snapshot to the kernel log as well.  This is a diagnostic-only
change: it selects where the already-captured text is written and does
not alter the capture path, the register reads, or any display
behaviour.

Cost is bounded.  Both triggers are once-per-boot guarded
(frame_done_timeout_cnt == 1 and fault_snapshot_capture == 0), so at
most a couple of dumps of a few thousand lines each per boot.  The
snapshot is also still handed to dev_coredumpm() exactly as before.

Context: chasing an intermittent top-panel corruption on the AYN Thor
(SM8550, DSI-2 1080x1920@120Hz, enc38).  At 120Hz the frame-done
watchdog budget is ~42ms against ~83ms at 60Hz, and the fault appears
only at 120Hz, so the DPU state at the moment of the missed frame-done
interrupt is the measurement that matters.  Symptom:

	dpu_encoder_frame_done_timeout: enc38 frame done timeout
	_dpu_encoder_phys_cmd_handle_ppdone_timeout: id:38 pp:2 kickoff timeout 1 cnt 1 koff_cnt 1
	dpu_encoder_phys_cmd_prepare_for_kickoff: failed wait_for_idle: id:38 ret:-110 pp:2

Note that wait_for_idle() waits on the PINGPONG tx-done interrupt, so
-ETIMEDOUT there means the pingpong never completed the transfer.  This
patch does not attempt to fix that; it only makes the hardware state at
that moment observable instead of discarded.

Tested by inspection only.  No AYN Thor hardware was available to the
author, so the behavioural claim is taken from the device journal, and
the console output itself is unverified.

---
 drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/msm_disp_snapshot.h | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/msm_disp_snapshot.h
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/msm_disp_snapshot.h
@@ -29,7 +29,7 @@
 #define MSM_DISP_SNAPSHOT_MAX_BLKS		10

 /* debug option to print the registers in logs */
-#define MSM_DISP_SNAPSHOT_DUMP_IN_CONSOLE 0
+#define MSM_DISP_SNAPSHOT_DUMP_IN_CONSOLE 1

 /* print debug ranges in groups of 4 u32s */
 #define REG_DUMP_ALIGN		16
